    <h1 class="cc-title">CellClock</h1>
    <div class="cc-sub">Biological programming for longevity research</div>
    <p class="cc-hook">A home laboratory experiment exploring whether synthetic genetic circuits can influence how long cells live — and what that means for the future of biological computation.</p>
  </div>
</section>
<hr class="div">
<section class="sec reveal">
  <div class="sec-label">The question</div>
  <h2 class="sec-title">Can we reprogram how long a cell lives?</h2>
  <p class="body-text">Every cell has an internal clock. It divides a certain number of times — then it stops. In yeast, that number is roughly 25–30 divisions. In human cells, it's called the Hayflick limit. The question that drives CellClock: <strong>what if we could change that number?</strong></p>
  <p class="body-text">Not by brute force. Not by suppressing the signals that stop division — that's how cancer works. But by <strong>engineering the underlying genetic circuits</strong> that control the rhythm of aging itself. By creating a synthetic oscillator — a biological pendulum — that keeps the cell's internal clock running differently.</p>
  <p class="body-text">A 2023 paper published in <em>Science</em> showed this was possible: a synthetic genetic oscillator extended yeast replicative lifespan by <strong>82%</strong>. CellClock is built to replicate, verify, and extend that result.</p>
  <div class="paper-card">
    <div class="paper-label">Source · Science, 2023</div>
    <div class="paper-title">Synthetic gene network engineering induces longevity in yeast cells via lifespan extension</div>
    <div class="paper-desc">The paper demonstrated that a synthetic genetic oscillator — switching between two aging pathways — could prevent cells from becoming locked in senescent states, significantly extending replicative lifespan without suppressing natural cell death mechanisms.</div>
    <div class="paper-stat">+82% <span>replicative lifespan extension in S. cerevisiae</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class="stats-row">
    <div class="stat-box"><div class="stat-val"><em>82%</em></div><div class="stat-lbl">Lifespan extension shown in source paper</div></div>
    <div class="stat-box"><div class="stat-val">25–30</div><div class="stat-lbl">Normal yeast replicative divisions</div></div>
    <div class="stat-box"><div class="stat-val"><em>AI</em></div><div class="stat-lbl">Automated tracking via computer vision</div></div>
  </div>
</section>
<hr class="div">
<section class="sec reveal">
  <div class="sec-label">The method</div>
  <h2 class="sec-title">How it works.</h2>
  <p class="body-text">CellClock uses baker's yeast — <em>Saccharomyces cerevisiae</em> — as the model organism. Yeast shares fundamental aging mechanisms with human cells and is ideal for genetic experiments.</p>
  <p class="body-text">The experiment introduces a <strong>synthetic genetic oscillator</strong> into the yeast using CRISPR — a molecular tool that allows precise edits to DNA. A computer vision pipeline built in Python and OpenCV automatically detects individual cells, tracks them across frames, and counts division events.</p>
  <div class="bio-visual">
    <div class="bv-item"><span class="bv-icon">🧫</span><div class="bv-name">S. cerevisiae</div><div class="bv-desc">Baker's yeast. Shares fundamental aging mechanisms with human cells. Safe and ideal for genetic experiments.</div></div>
    <div class="bv-item"><span class="bv-icon">✂️</span><div class="bv-name">CRISPR Editing</div><div class="bv-desc">Precise DNA editing to introduce the synthetic oscillator circuit into the yeast genome.</div></div>
    <div class="bv-item"><span class="bv-icon">🟢</span><div class="bv-name">GFP Tagging</div><div class="bv-desc">Green Fluorescent Protein enables automated cell tracking and division counting via computer vision.</div></div>
  </div>
  <div class="four-col">
    <div class="col-item"><div class="col-label">Step 1 — Setup</div><div class="col-text">GFP-tagged yeast grown on YPD agar. Control and experimental groups prepared in parallel.</div></div>
    <div class="col-item"><div class="col-label">Step 2 — Observation</div><div class="col-text">USB microscope captures continuous time-lapse footage over the full replicative lifespan.</div></div>
    <div class="col-item"><div class="col-label">Step 3 — Tracking</div><div class="col-text">Python + OpenCV pipeline automatically detects cells and counts division events per mother cell.</div></div>
    <div class="col-item"><div class="col-label">Step 4 — Analysis</div><div class="col-text">Replicative lifespan compared between control and experimental groups with statistical analysis.</div></div>
  </div>
</section>
<hr class="div">
<section class="sec reveal">
  <div class="sec-label">Roadmap</div>
  <h2 class="sec-title">Phase by phase.</h2>
  <div class="phase"><div class="phase-n">Phase 1</div><div><div class="phase-title">Lab setup & baseline measurement</div><div class="phase-desc">Establish GFP-yeast culture. Calibrate USB microscope. Build and test OpenCV tracking pipeline. Measure control group lifespan to establish baseline.</div></div><span class="badge b-now">Active</span></div>
  <div class="phase"><div class="phase-n">Phase 2</div><div><div class="phase-title">CRISPR introduction & replication</div><div class="phase-desc">Introduce synthetic oscillator via CRISPR. Run parallel experiment alongside control. Replicate the 2023 Science paper result independently.</div></div><span class="badge b-next">Next</span></div>
  <div class="phase"><div class="phase-n">Phase 3</div><div><div class="phase-title">AI-guided intervention</div><div class="phase-desc">Train a computer vision model on division patterns. Use AI to identify early senescence. Test whether targeted intervention can extend lifespan further.</div></div><span class="badge b-future">Planned</span></div>
  <div class="phase"><div class="phase-n">Phase 4</div><div><div class="phase-title">Closed-loop autonomous system</div><div class="phase-desc">A fully closed-loop system where AI observes biological state, designs an intervention, delivers it, and measures the outcome — without human input.</div></div><span class="badge b-future">Vision</span></div>
</section>
<hr class="div">
<section class="sec reveal">
  <div class="sec-label">The bigger picture</div>
  <h2 class="sec-title">Why this connects to everything else.</h2>
  <p class="body-text">CellClock and NeuroBridge look like different projects. One is software infrastructure. One is a biology experiment. But they share the same underlying question: <strong>how does nature compute, and what can we learn from it?</strong></p>
  <div class="connection-card">
    <div class="conn-label">The connection</div>
    <div class="conn-text">Neuromorphic chips are inspired by how neurons process information — <strong>efficiently, in spikes, only when needed</strong>. Biological genetic circuits operate the same way — oscillating, switching, responding to state. CellClock is a study of biological computation in its most fundamental form. NeuroBridge is an attempt to build artificial systems that work the same way. <strong>They are the same idea at different scales.</strong></div>
  </div>
